From 014ea5d072eeae0655d559eb007e3197968688ac Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Mateusz Bajorski <mbajorski@shasta.cloud>
Date: Fri, 22 May 2026 17:41:43 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] gen-loc-config: hw-id binding for every VIF, not just LMP
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Generalizes the deterministic-MAC mechanism patch 0009 introduced for the
LMP NIC so every virtual NIC handed to the VyOS guest is anchored to a
stable MAC and the matching hw-id config line is emitted in cloud-init.

Before: only HWIDs[<lmp_name>] = lmp_mac was populated; non-LMP VIFs
(uplink, switch NIs) had no MacAddress set in their NetworkAdapter
entries — domainmgr generated runtime MACs and no hw-id line was emitted,
so VyOS's view of those interfaces floated with kernel probe order. User
hit "sometimes WAN is not eth0 but eth1" on ms-01 because of this.

After: every VIF gets a deterministic MAC via vifMACFromUUIDs(appUUID,
niUUID, adapterNum) — adapterNum 0=LMP, 1=uplink, 2+ = switch NIs. The
NetworkAdapter.MacAddress field carries it to domainmgr (so the guest
NIC has the expected MAC), and HWIDs[ethN] is populated for each
guest-side interface name. The cloud-init emitter (unchanged) writes
`set interfaces ethernet ethN hw-id '<mac>'` for every entry. VyOS now
anchors every interface's config to a MAC, not to whichever ethN the
kernel happened to assign.

lmpMACFromUUIDs is kept as a thin wrapper over vifMACFromUUIDs(_, _, 0)
for clarity at the LMP call site.

Hardware (PCIe-passthrough) NICs are still unsolved by this patch — their
vendor MACs aren't known at build time. Follow-up: discover them at boot
from /sys/bus/pci/.../net/<iface>/address and substitute into the
cloud-init template.

gen-loc-config: always enforce VIF interface order

Patch "hw-id binding for every VIF" assumed pillar/domainmgr would honour
the InterfaceOrder field on every NetworkAdapter so that LMP at order=1
lands at the lowest guest PCI slot (kernel eth0) and switch NIs at
order=2+ follow as eth1+. That is only true when
EnforceNetworkInterfaceOrder is set.

gen-loc-config previously gated it on `len(pcieAdapters) > 0`, so the
qemu profile (no PCIe) got it `false` — pillar then placed the switch
NI at the lower PCI slot, the LMP at the higher one, and the VyOS hw-id
config (which the previous patch built from InterfaceOrder) ended up
inverted vs kernel reality. VyOS's commit handler tried to reconcile
and corrupted kernel MACs (both ethN reporting the same address).

Setting EnforceNetworkInterfaceOrder unconditionally makes the
build-time HWIDs map match the runtime kernel naming. The qemu profile
now produces stable hw-id bindings on both eth0 and eth1.

gen-loc-config: drop hw-id cidata directives

The hw-id-for-every-VIF logic emitted `set interfaces ethernet <iface>
hw-id '<mac>'` for every interface in VyOS cidata. VyOS's
[interfaces_ethernet] validator rejects this on bridge members at commit
time:

  Cannot assign address to interface "eth0" as it is a member of bridge

That made first-boot cidata commit fail (alongside a separate transient
"NAT translation requires address" warning), leaving VyOS with a
half-applied config: br0 configured but DOWN, eth0 stuck with no IP,
network unreachable until manual repair.

The whole hw-id mechanism is redundant in EVE's current setup:

  1. EnforceNetworkInterfaceOrder=true (set unconditionally just below)
     pins each VIF to a deterministic PCI bus slot via VifOrder, so the
     same VM config always lands at the same pci.N addresses across
     reboots.
  2. VyOS's live-build kernel cmdline carries `net.ifnames=0
     biosdevname=0`, so the kernel uses legacy ethN names in PCI
     enumeration order.

Together those guarantee `eth0 = first VIF, eth1 = second, ...`
unconditionally — VyOS sees stable names without any hw-id config.

Drop the emission, drop the now-unused `HWIDs` map field, drop its
population. First-boot cidata apply now succeeds in one pass.

Signed-off-by: Mateusz Bajorski <mbajorski@shasta.cloud>
---
 pkg/pillar/cmd/gen-loc-config/main.go | 119 +++++++++++++++-----------
 1 file changed, 69 insertions(+), 50 deletions(-)

diff --git a/pkg/pillar/cmd/gen-loc-config/main.go b/pkg/pillar/cmd/gen-loc-config/main.go
index f27c0503a..5bd911dec 100644
--- a/pkg/pillar/cmd/gen-loc-config/main.go
+++ b/pkg/pillar/cmd/gen-loc-config/main.go
@@ -75,12 +75,10 @@ type VyOSConfig struct {
 	CIOutput    string   `yaml:"ci_output"`    // output path for the cloud-init ISO
 	Commands    []string `yaml:"commands"`     // additional VyOS set commands appended last
 	// LMPName is the interface name VyOS will use for the LMP virtio NIC.
-	// VyOS renames the NIC (via a udev hw-id rule) to this name on boot.
-	// Defaults to "eth100" if not set.
+	// Kernel-side ethN naming is determined by PCI bus order (anchored by
+	// EVE's EnforceNetworkInterfaceOrder) plus VyOS's `net.ifnames=0
+	// biosdevname=0` kernel cmdline. Defaults to "eth100" if not set.
 	LMPName string `yaml:"lmp_name"`
-	// HWIDs maps VyOS interface names to MAC addresses for hw-id binding.
-	// Populated automatically by gen-loc-config; no need to set in the profile.
-	HWIDs map[string]string `yaml:"-"`
 }
 
 // Profile describes a compile-time machine configuration loaded from a YAML file.
@@ -142,11 +140,17 @@ func generateVyOSUserData(vyosCfg *VyOSConfig) string {
 		add("set system host-name '" + v.Hostname + "'")
 	}
 
-	// hw-id bindings: pin each interface name to its MAC address so VyOS
-	// finds the right NIC regardless of PCI enumeration order.
-	for iface, mac := range v.HWIDs {
-		add("set interfaces ethernet " + iface + " hw-id '" + mac + "'")
-	}
+	// Deterministic ethN naming is guaranteed by two existing mechanisms:
+	//   1. EVE's `EnforceNetworkInterfaceOrder=true` (set unconditionally
+	//      below) makes pciAddressAllocator pin each VIF to a fixed PCI
+	//      bus slot per VifOrder — same VM config → same slots every boot.
+	//   2. VyOS's kernel cmdline carries `net.ifnames=0 biosdevname=0`,
+	//      so the kernel uses legacy ethN names assigned by PCI bus order.
+	// We previously emitted `set interfaces ethernet <iface> hw-id '<mac>'`
+	// here as belt-and-suspenders, but VyOS rejects it on bridge members
+	// at commit time ("Cannot assign address to interface as it is a
+	// member of bridge"), which broke first-boot cidata apply. Dropping
+	// hw-id everywhere removes the conflict and the cidata commits cleanly.
 
 	// WAN interface: DHCP client
 	add("set interfaces ethernet " + v.WAN + " description 'WAN'")
@@ -346,7 +350,8 @@ func main() {
 	// (virtio: 0x00:XX.0, passthrough: host PCI addr e.g. 0x02:00.0), so inside the guest
 	// Linux names them eth0, eth1, ... ahead of the PCIe NICs.
 	// LMP is always placed first (InterfaceOrder=1) so it deterministically gets eth0
-	// inside the guest, where VyOS renames it to eth100 via hw-id binding.
+	// inside the guest. VyOS does not rename it — `LMPName` is informational only,
+	// kept in the profile for documentation alignment.
 	// Other VirtIO NICs (uplink, switch NIs) follow at InterfaceOrder len(pcieAdapters)+2 onward.
 	var appInterfaces []*zconfig.NetworkAdapter
 
@@ -369,36 +374,34 @@ func main() {
 				},
 			},
 		})
-		if vyosCfg != nil {
-			if vyosCfg.HWIDs == nil {
-				vyosCfg.HWIDs = make(map[string]string)
-			}
-			lmpVyOSName := vyosCfg.LMPName
-			if lmpVyOSName == "" {
-				lmpVyOSName = "eth100"
-			}
-			vyosCfg.HWIDs[lmpVyOSName] = lmpMAC.String()
-		}
 	}
 
 	// Non-LMP VirtIO NICs follow after LMP (slot 1) and PCIe slots (2..len+1).
+	// Each VIF gets a deterministic MAC derived from (appUUID, niUUID, adapterNum)
+	// for stable hashing across boots. Kernel-side ethN naming is anchored by
+	// the PCI bus order (set by EnforceNetworkInterfaceOrder) plus VyOS's
+	// `net.ifnames=0 biosdevname=0` kernel cmdline — VyOS does not need any
+	// hw-id directives in its config to track names.
+	// adapterNum allocation:  0 = LMP, 1 = uplink, 2+ = switch NIs (in profile order)
 	virtIntfOrder := uint32(len(pcieAdapters) + 2)
 
-	if *uplink != "" {
+	addVIF := func(niUUID string, adapterNum uint8) {
+		vifMAC := vifMACFromUUIDs(uuid.FromStringOrNil(appUUID), uuid.FromStringOrNil(niUUID), adapterNum)
+		ethName := fmt.Sprintf("eth%d", virtIntfOrder-1)
 		appInterfaces = append(appInterfaces, &zconfig.NetworkAdapter{
-			Name:           fmt.Sprintf("eth%d", virtIntfOrder-1),
-			NetworkId:      natNIUUID,
+			Name:           ethName,
+			NetworkId:      niUUID,
 			InterfaceOrder: virtIntfOrder,
+			MacAddress:     vifMAC.String(),
 		})
 		virtIntfOrder++
 	}
-	for _, sni := range switchNIs {
-		appInterfaces = append(appInterfaces, &zconfig.NetworkAdapter{
-			Name:           fmt.Sprintf("eth%d", virtIntfOrder-1),
-			NetworkId:      sni.niUUID,
-			InterfaceOrder: virtIntfOrder,
-		})
-		virtIntfOrder++
+
+	if *uplink != "" {
+		addVIF(natNIUUID, 1)
+	}
+	for i, sni := range switchNIs {
+		addVIF(sni.niUUID, uint8(i+2))
 	}
 
 	// DeviceIoList: tell EVE about PCIe-assignable adapters.
@@ -500,15 +503,22 @@ func main() {
 				Uuidandversion: &zconfig.UUIDandVersion{Uuid: appUUID, Version: "1"},
 				Displayname:    *name,
 				Fixedresources: &zconfig.VmConfig{
-					Memory:                       uint32(*memoryMB * 1024),
-					Maxmem:                       uint32(*memoryMB * 1024),
-					Vcpus:                        uint32(*cpus),
-					Maxcpus:                      uint32(*cpus),
-					VirtualizationMode:           zconfig.VmMode_HVM,
-					EnableVnc:                    *enableVNC,
-					VncDisplay:                   1,
-					VncPasswd:                    *vncPasswd,
-					EnforceNetworkInterfaceOrder: len(pcieAdapters) > 0,
+					Memory:             uint32(*memoryMB * 1024),
+					Maxmem:             uint32(*memoryMB * 1024),
+					Vcpus:              uint32(*cpus),
+					Maxcpus:            uint32(*cpus),
+					VirtualizationMode: zconfig.VmMode_HVM,
+					EnableVnc:          *enableVNC,
+					VncDisplay:         1,
+					VncPasswd:          *vncPasswd,
+					// Always enforce interface order so guest-kernel ethN naming is
+					// deterministic across reboots. When this is false, pillar/domainmgr
+					// free-orders the NICs and the LMP can end up at kernel eth1 instead
+					// of eth0 (observed on qemu profile). With it true, pciAddressAllocator
+					// pins each VIF to a PCI bus slot derived from VifOrder, and VyOS's
+					// `net.ifnames=0 biosdevname=0` cmdline ensures kernel names follow
+					// bus order.
+					EnforceNetworkInterfaceOrder: true,
 				},
 				VolumeRefList: []*zconfig.VolumeRef{
 					{Uuid: volUUID, GenerationCount: 0, MountDir: ""},
@@ -568,22 +578,31 @@ func main() {
 	}
 }
 
-// lmpMACFromUUIDs computes a deterministic MAC address for the LMP virtual NIC.
-// The MAC is derived from the app and LMP network-instance UUIDs using SHA-256,
-// matching the OUI (02:16:3E) that EVE uses for switch-NI virtual NICs.
-// Unlike EVE's runtime MAC generation it does not include the per-device AppNum,
-// so the result is stable across reinstalls and independent of runtime state.
-// Setting this MAC explicitly in the NetworkAdapter config causes EVE to use it
-// instead of its own generated value.
-func lmpMACFromUUIDs(appUUID, lmpNIUUID uuid.UUID) net.HardwareAddr {
+// vifMACFromUUIDs computes a deterministic MAC address for a virtual NIC
+// attached to the VyOS guest. The MAC is derived from (appUUID, niUUID,
+// adapterNum) using SHA-256, matching the OUI (02:16:3E) that EVE uses for
+// switch-NI virtual NICs. Unlike EVE's runtime MAC generation it does not
+// include the per-device AppNum, so the result is stable across reinstalls
+// and independent of runtime state. Setting this MAC explicitly in the
+// NetworkAdapter config causes EVE to use it instead of generating one.
+//
+// adapterNum disambiguates multiple NICs that share an niUUID and keeps each
+// MAC unique within the same app. By convention 0=LMP, 1=uplink, 2+ = switch
+// NIs in profile order.
+func vifMACFromUUIDs(appUUID, niUUID uuid.UUID, adapterNum uint8) net.HardwareAddr {
 	h := sha256.New()
 	h.Write(appUUID[:])
-	h.Write(lmpNIUUID[:])
-	h.Write([]byte{0}) // adapterNum = 0 (LMP is the only virtual NIC)
+	h.Write(niUUID[:])
+	h.Write([]byte{adapterNum})
 	hash := h.Sum(nil)
 	return net.HardwareAddr{0x02, 0x16, 0x3e, hash[0], hash[1], hash[2]}
 }
 
+// lmpMACFromUUIDs is a backward-compatible wrapper for the LMP VIF (adapterNum=0).
+func lmpMACFromUUIDs(appUUID, lmpNIUUID uuid.UUID) net.HardwareAddr {
+	return vifMACFromUUIDs(appUUID, lmpNIUUID, 0)
+}
